    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d5">

                <pb facs="0054" n="4" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav03/pg4"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.51/135</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Russia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Francis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Petrograd</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1917-04-21T22:00:00+02:31"><hi rendition="#italic">April 21, 1917,
                                10 p.m.</hi></date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 11 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1212. For the Secretary of the Treasury:</p>
                <p>Minister of Finance states total annual interest Rs. 1,400,000,000; total funded
                    debt Rs. 37,000,000,000, of which Rs. 1,000,000,000 mature <pb facs="0055" n="5"
                        xml:id="pg5"/> in 1921, Rs. 1,000,000,000 in 1925, and Rs. 2,000,000,000 in
                    1926; remainder are 49-year bonds maturing in 1963. Says state holdings of lands
                    and forests and deposits of coal, petroleum, platinum, and gold aggregate much
                    more [than] total indebtedness. Pokrovski, late Minister for Foreign Affairs and
                    former Comptroller of the Empire, is now employed by Government in appraising
                    Russia’s assets. Shall cable report when finished. My judgment is that Russia
                    can undoubtedly meet all obligations. What Minister of Finance most desires is
                    long time, and would pay 4½ per cent or even 5 per cent therefor. Says
                    confidentially would like to transfer from England to us the $360,000,000
                    mentioned in my No. 1204, but without taking same out of our loan to Russia
                    which should be at least equal to that of England and France (see my telegram
                    1211 to Lansing concerning
                        conditions<anchor n="1" xml:id="d5-f1"/>).</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Francis</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
